3. What is the difference between a prospective and a retrospective study?

   Answer: The difference lies in the direction of data collection over time. A prospective study observes the exposure, then collects data. A retrospective study first observes outcomes, then looks backwards to see what exposures the groups have experienced. A cohort study can be either prospective or retrospective, while a case-control study is always retrospective. For example, suppose we are interested in the effect of pre-kindergarten (pre-K) studies on Grade 12 performance. 
   
   - A prospective cohort study first identifies two groups of kids (those who go to pre-K vs those who do not), then observes them from kindergarten age until they are Grade 12 students. This can be done by tracking the students’ grades from Grade 1 to Grade 12. 
   - A retrospective cohort study also identifies the same two groups of students, but observations are done from present to past. In this case, we would track students’ grades from Grade 12 all the way to Grade 1. We do not spend 13-15 years observing students’ performances, as opposed to a prospective cohort study.
   - A case-control study focuses on Grade 12 only. We create two groups of recent high school graduates (those who excelled in high school vs those who struggled), then “look back” to determine if the excelling group has more students who received pre-K education. We are not interested in students’ performances from Grade 1 to Grade 11, as opposed to cohort studies.

4. Are interventional studies stronger than observational studies?

   Answer: No. Interventional studies can sometimes be better than observational studies in terms of causal inference. That said, observational studies use inductive reasoning to make cases for causation. As you know, induction is stronger if it is based on multiple lines of evidence. Plate tectonics theory, the most important theory in all of the Earth sciences, was developed through a combination of many quality observational studies. In addition, because observational studies tend to use data “from the real world” (as opposed to a tightly controlled laboratory), their results are often more generalizable than those of interventional studies.

5. When should we prioritize using observational studies over interventional studies?

   Answer: Observational studies are better when it is challenging to implement an intervention. This condition usually happens in two scenarios:
   
   - Logistical constraints: It may be too expensive to carry out an intervention, or the temporal scale is beyond a human’s average lifespan. For example, an interventional study is more suitable to study the evolution of a bacteria species since their lifespans are short, and it doesn’t cost much to create a chemical stimulant. In contrast, an observational study is more suitable for the evolution of tortoises, which can live for more than 100 years. It also might just be impossible to perform an intervention. For example, we can’t manually adjust the rate of plate spreading and see how it affects earthquakes; we use what nature provides by observing different plate spreading rates and monitor the effect on earthquakes..
   - Ethical issues: Some interventions may raise ethical conflicts; observational studies can avoid them since they do not have interventions. For example, to study the negative effects of a an illegal drug, we would collect data on recreational users of the drug and compare to non-users.

